course: dictionary
lesson: 6241
title: Modal verbs
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A modal verb is a helping verb. It sits in front of a main verb and changes how sure, able, allowed or necessary the action is. The modal verbs are can, could, may, might, will, would, shall, should and must.
explain: Compare «You must tidy up» with «You could tidy up». Both use the same main verb, tidy, but must sounds like an order, because it is necessary. Could sounds like a gentle suggestion, because it is only possible.
explain: Verbs can work in teams. In «I can swim», can is the modal verb and swim is the main verb. Modal verbs never add an s or change their ending, so we say «he can» and «they can», not «he cans».
fact: A modal verb is a helping verb that shows how sure, able, allowed or necessary something is.
fact: Modal verbs such as can, could, may, might, will, would, shall, should and must sit in front of another verb.
fact: «I can sing and I will dance» has four verbs: can and will are modal verbs, and sing and dance are main verbs.
fact: «Must» shows something is necessary, «might» shows it is possible, and «can» shows it is allowed or someone is able to do it.
fact: «Modal» is also an adjective, so you can have a modal verb, and in maths the modal value is the one that appears most often.
exercise: In «I can swim», which word is the modal verb? | *can | I | swim
exercise: Which sentence sounds like an order? | You could tidy up | *You must tidy up | You might tidy up
exercise: Which sentence is correct? | He cans swim | He can swims | He cans swims | *He can swim
test: In «I will dance», which word is the modal verb? | dance | *will | I
test: Which modal verb shows that something is possible, not certain? | must | shall | *might | should
exam: In «I can sing and I will dance», how many verbs are there? | Three | Two | *Four | Five
